    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians.jpg" alt="Géza, Grand Prince of the Hungarians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians">Géza, Grand Prince of the Hungarians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geza-ChroniconPictum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Márk Kálti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> was created by the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> principality that had occupied the Carpathian basin from about 895. Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians">Géza</a> (r. c. 972–997) made peace with the Ottonian emperors, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> missionaries and had himself and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Vajk</a> baptised; the son took the name Stephen. Stephen married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisela-of-bavaria">Gisela of Bavaria</a>, sister of the future Emperor Henry II, and on his father's death in 997 defeated his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koppany">Koppány</a>, who claimed the succession by seniority. He was crowned king at Christmas 1000 or on 1 January 1001, with a crown that, according to his later legends, was sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Pope Sylvester II</a> with the approval of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a>. Stephen then extended his rule over the semi-independent chiefs of the basin: he overthrew his maternal uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-the-younger">Gyula</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> around 1003 and later, probably in the 1020s, destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahtum">Ahtum</a>, who ruled the lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mures-river">Maros</a>. He founded the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> and a second at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalocsa">Kalocsa</a>, with about eight further bishoprics, and supported Benedictine abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonhalma-archabbey">Pannonhalma</a>, begun under Géza in 996. By his death in 1038 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was a Christian kingdom fully part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>, though his only surviving son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emeric-of-hungary">Emeric</a> had died in 1031, opening decades of succession struggles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/esztergom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esztergom.jpg" alt="Esztergom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EsztergomMontage2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">see above, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Árpádian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was a thinly settled country, with perhaps one to two million people around 1200, whose economy combined stock-raising inherited from the nomadic past with growing grain agriculture and village settlement. The king was by far the greatest landowner, and royal estates, worked by many grades of dependants and unfree servants, supplied the court as it travelled. Salt from Transylvanian mines, tolls and the minting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, begun under Stephen I, provided royal income. Society was ethnically mixed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> served as frontier warriors, Muslim and Jewish merchants handled trade and sometimes royal finances, and from the twelfth century Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloons">Walloons</a> and Italians were invited as ‘guests’ (<em>hospites</em>) with their own liberties; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-ii-of-hungary">Géza II</a> settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Saxons of Transylvania</a>, whose rights <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a> confirmed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-andreanum">Andreanum</a> of 1224. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread slowly: Stephen ordered every ten villages to build a church, and pagan revolts broke out in 1046 and 1061. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> grew as trading and royal centres, and silver mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banska-stiavnica">Selmecbánya</a> (Banská Štiavnica) expanded in the thirteenth century. A contemporary list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III</a>'s revenues presents an income rivalling that of western kings, though its figures are disputed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld.jpg" alt="Battle of Lechfeld" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lechfeld1457.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hektor Mülich[1] (d. 1490), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> had raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> until their defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955, and light horsemen remained part of Hungarian armies, but Stephen I and his successors also adopted western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, brought in partly by German knights. The core forces were the royal retinue and the castle warriors of the counties, later joined by the nobles' own followers. The kingdom defended itself successfully against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II</a>'s invasion of 1030 failed, and although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> restored King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-menfo">Battle of Ménfő</a> in 1044, his campaigns of 1051 and 1052, including a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, ended in failure. Expansion went south. Ladislaus I occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> around 1091, and Coloman conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and the Dalmatian towns, being crowned king of Croatia in 1102. Wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> ended in defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sirmium">Battle of Sirmium</a> in 1167, and repeated campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> brought no lasting gains. The Mongol invasion exposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s weakness: on 11 April 1241 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a>'s army destroyed the royal host at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Battle of Mohi</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajo">Sajó</a> river. Afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV</a> encouraged stone castles, which helped repel a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, and in 1278 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV</a>'s cavalry helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Marchfeld</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1222" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:II._Andr%C3%A1s_aranybull%C3%A1ja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Stephen I organised the realm into royal counties, each centred on a castle and headed by an <em>ispán</em> (count), who commanded the castle warriors, collected revenues and judged in the king's name; their number grew to several dozen by the thirteenth century. The palatine, the highest royal officer, deputised for the king in justice. Stephen's laws protected property and the church, and his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admonitions-of-saint-stephen">Admonitions</a></em> to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emeric-of-hungary">Emeric</a> set out an ideal of Christian kingship that welcomed foreign settlers. Succession was disputed between seniority and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, leading to repeated wars between brothers, uncles and nephews in the eleventh century. Ladislaus I and Coloman issued further law codes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III</a> (r. 1172–1196), raised at the Byzantine court, developed a royal chancery and the use of written charters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a> (r. 1205–1235) gave away royal estates on a large scale, angering the lesser landholders, the royal servants, who forced him to issue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a>. It guaranteed their freedom from arbitrary arrest and from taxes on their estates, barred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Muslims from royal financial offices and allowed resistance to a king who broke its terms. After 1241 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV</a> granted land and castle-building rights to nobles to secure defence, which strengthened the great baronial families who dominated the kingdom's last decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Holy Crown of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Parliament_002_-_Flickr_-_granada_turnier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">granada_turnier, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Árpádian kingdom fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a> and set institutions that lasted into modern times. Stephen I was canonised in 1083, together with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-emeric-of-hungary">Emeric</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad">Gerard of Csanád</a>, and Ladislaus I in 1192; the dynasty became known for its saints, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a>'s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Elizabeth of Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV</a>'s daughter Margaret. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown</a>, later associated with Stephen though its surviving form dates from the later eleventh and twelfth centuries, became the symbol of the kingdom and of its political community. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a>, issued seven years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, became a cornerstone of noble liberties and was confirmed by later kings. The union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in 1102 lasted, in changing forms, until 1918, and the settlement of Germans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the towns shaped the region's ethnic mix for centuries. The kingdom acted as the eastern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, absorbing the Mongol blow of 1241 and settling and converting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and other steppe peoples. Its chronicles, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a></em> written around 1200 by an anonymous notary of a King Béla, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-of-keza">Simon of Kézai</a>'s <em>Gesta</em> of the 1280s, shaped Hungarian historical memory for centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony.jpg" alt="Battle of Rozgony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony">Battle of Rozgony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rozgony_Battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Márk Kálti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Árpádian monarchy weakened in its last decades not through foreign conquest but through the loss of royal land and the rise of great lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV</a> quarrelled with his son, the future Stephen V, and the two divided the kingdom between them in the 1260s. Stephen V died in 1272, leaving the ten-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-iv-of-hungary">Ladislaus IV</a>, whose minority let baronial factions fight over the regency. Ladislaus, raised among his mother's Cuman kin, alienated the church and nobles and was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> in 1290. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-iii-of-hungary">Andrew III</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a> born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, faced rival claimants backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and real power in many regions passed to oligarchs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-iii-csak">Matthew Csák</a> in the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-aba">Amadeus Aba</a> in the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-kan">Ladislaus Kán</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the Kőszegi family in the west. When Andrew III died on 14 January 1301 the male line of the Árpád dynasty ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles Robert of Anjou</a>, a descendant through the female line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus of Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-duke-of-bavaria">Otto of Bavaria</a> all claimed the throne. Charles, crowned in 1301, 1309 and finally with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown</a> in 1310, prevailed and founded the Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, though he needed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony">Battle of Rozgony</a> in 1312 and further campaigns to defeat the oligarchs.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Árpádian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> turned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> principality into a Christian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>, building royal counties, the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and the noble liberties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a>, and survived the Mongol invasion of 1241 before baronial power and the end of the dynasty in 1301 opened the Angevin era.</em></p>

